WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:08.990
Measure W asks voters in one area of this high school district whether to approve $33.8 million in school bonds.

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:15.430
Only voters in that area vote, and a tax on property there would repay the bonds.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:19.650
Measure W needs 55 percent of the vote to pass.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:27.310
The money would modernize classrooms, make safety improvements, replace leaky roofs, and renovate plumbing.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:34.250
The money could not pay salaries, and yearly audits and a citizens' committee would oversee the spending.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:46.770
The district's tax statement estimates an average tax of $29.43 per $100,000 of assessed value each year, through 2060-2061.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:55.280
The statement puts the total to repay, with interest, at about $68,850,000.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:04.410
Supporters say many classrooms and buildings no longer meet modern standards, and passing qualifies the district for state matching funds.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:11.770
No argument against Measure W appears among the documents that the county elections office has posted for this election.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:20.480
A yes vote authorizes the district to issue up to $33.8 million in bonds, repaid by a property tax in that area.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:24.790
A no vote is against authorizing the district to issue the bonds.
